    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Without question, you know, historically, you know, the two parties, major parties, had philosophical differences about a lot of things. The Republican Party has become, was hijacked by Donald Trump and fear is a powerful thing and he imposes himself on a process that doesn't understand. It has been very, very difficult. We, as a nation, came very close to losing our democracy on January 6th. There was a plan that could have thrown that election to the House of Representatives, not without a legal basis, but there were ways that they were trying to accomplish that. And when it's an amendment to 12 that would throw the election, if they were able to bring Biden's electoral votes to below 270, Article Amendment 12 could have been invoked. And it's called a contingent election. And it would have gone to the House of Representatives. It wasn't, it would not have been a one person, one vote situation or one state, one vote. And Republicans had control of 26 states to Democrats 22. That was the plan. So Trump wanted Pence to invoke a constitutional power that he didn't have. But it could have gone far enough where they could have pulled it off. And I just had my good friend and colleague Jamie Raskin in Buffalo last week who wrote a book. He lost his son to suicide and then subsequently led the second impeachment of Trump. And it was a violent insurrection, and it was a coup attempt. What you would prefer in political science terms is a self-cool, not a sitting president being overtaken by the military, but a president trying to hang on. It's a problem, and my concern is, it's everything's friended and redistinctions. Everything is erosum.",
