  {
    "episode_id": 44764,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44764.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "go out there, I'm driving like through Lancaster and think, what am I doing out here, right? So this guy's name is like, so I said, like, why are you out here? He said, well, you know, Martin Luther King Park, there was a shooting there last week. So when people from the community want to utilize a park to celebrate their annual reunion and they're going out to Lancaster when in fact we have MLK Park here that's a problem that's a problem and I think you know you look at MLK Park it could we could have a lot more trees we could have all the parks you know they're good for the good that they do but I think you know when you look at a community and neighborhood that's was ravaged by this shooting Obviously, life quality issues of education, of housing, of infrastructure, of equity, of opportunity, inclusion are very, very important. And we have to get this right. I will also say this. I'm surprised that there wasn't a lot of violence after May 14th, and I attribute that to the faith leaders, to a lot of good people who I knew, but now know in a different way because of the leadership that they demonstrated. They didn't try to sugarcoat anything. This was a white nationalist who came into Buffalo from far outside of our community with one objective, to kill black people. that could have triggered something that was much worse. I think the political community, I think the mayor did a very good job. I think his demeanor was very, very helpful in this. Again, not attempting to say, you know, this is not as bad. It was bad. It doesn't get any worse. But to also say, we're going to be okay. And I saw that in many of the vigils, many of the meetings with faith community, elected officials, et cetera. So I think Buffalo responded as best it could. But you've got to find a way to honor the memories of those good people. that were killed. And the best way to do that is to create a healthier, more inclusive neighborhood that they were all committed to. These were good people. They were fathers and mothers. They were grandparents and retired school teacher, retired police officer. So there's the lesson. It should be a rallying point. to demand more, yes, but to make sure that new element of help that's coming into the community is used to achieve the highest and best purpose.",
